WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.250
A calendar's pages fly forward,
from 1895 into the far future.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.940
Above ground, gentle people
stroll through gardens.

3
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.100
Below, in the dark, others
tend humming machines.

4
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:18.010
The traveller's theory: "above
ground you must have the Haves,

5
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.080
pursuing pleasure and comfort and beauty,

6
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:26.400
and below ground the Have-nots," So why
does this far future feel like the writer's

7
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:27.920
own present?

8
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.180
In this mission, you'll learn
the machinery of dystopia,

9
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.210
from surveillance to the
figure who questions.

10
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.720
You'll hold a lens over his future, and
ask what it has to do with 1890s London.

11
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.710
And you'll set other dystopias
beside their own dates,

12
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.250
and ask what their writers were watching.

13
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.540
Your mission: explain why dystopias seem
to be about the writer's own present.
